      "provenance": {
        "notes": "Genus-level entry for garden roses (starter row 55): patio and miniature roses are what the pot guidance covers; shrub, climbing and rambler roses differ a lot (RHS: climbers and ground cover need pots at least 30 to 45 cm deep; Gardeners' World advice that ramblers are best not potted is search-summary only, not cited). Watering: RHS growing guide says container roses need much more water than border roses, as often as needed, up to daily in hot weather, never dry or waterlogged; the container page agrees (dry pots get powdery mildew). Interval 2 days is a guess. Light: RHS at least 4 hours of sun a day (container page: at least half the day); NC State full sun; stored direct. Pots: patio and miniature roses in 23 to 35 cm deep pots, peat-free John Innes No 3, feed every fortnight from mid-spring to late summer. Size and growth rate left unknown: RHS gives no figures for the group; NC State's genus range (30 to 240 cm tall, 60 to 180 wide) is too wide to store, and the NC State page was truncated by the fetch. Habit bushy is a mapping (NC State: shrubs or vines). Hardiness: neither page gives a figure. Toxicity: ASPCA lists Rosa species non-toxic to cats and dogs; severity 0 is our grading. NC State not cited for pets (pet part not read). Prickles (not thorns, NC State) can hurt but there is no vocabulary entry for injury; not graded. Es and it names are machine drafts."
      },
